#What does Big-O measures?

# Big-O tells us how the running time grows as the input size (n) becomes large.
# It focuses on:
# 1- number of comparisons
# 2- number of swaps
# 3- not real seconds.

# Structure of Bubble Sort and why it matters.

# Example of it: 
# Bubble sort uses two loops:

# for i in range(n):
#    for j in range(n - i - 1):
#        compare & maybe swap

# So for each pass, we compare many elements.
# Worst Case Time Complexity → O(n²)
# This happens when the list is in reverse order.

# Example:

# [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]

# Every comparison leads to a swap.

# Number of operations roughly becomes:

# n * n = n²

# So:

# Worst Case = O(n²)

# Average Case → O(n²)

# Even with random data, it still needs many comparisons.

# Average = O(n²)

# Best Case → depends
# Without optimization

# Even if already sorted, it still runs all loops.

#[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

# Best = O(n²)

# With early-stop optimization

# If we stop when no swaps happen → only 1 pass.

# Best = O(n)

# Space Complexity → O(1)

# Bubble sort sorts in place.

# No extra arrays.

#### Summary ####
# Worst Case: O(n²)
# Average Case: O(n²)
# Best Case: O(n²)  // or O(n) with optimization
# Space Complexity: O(1)
